---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../components/AuthorByline.astro';
import BreadcrumbNav from '../components/BreadcrumbNav.astro';
import FAQSection from '../components/FAQSection.astro';
import { authorship, dataset } from '../lib/schema';
import { BOOKING_URL } from '../site.config';

const DATE_PUBLISHED = '2026-07-28';
const DATE_MODIFIED = '2026-07-28';

const faqs = [
  { question: 'What does Medigap cover that Original Medicare doesn\'t?', answer: 'Medigap covers some or all of the out-of-pocket costs left over from Original Medicare, such as Part A and Part B deductibles, coinsurance, and copayments, depending on the plan letter you choose.' },
  { question: 'What\'s the difference between Plan G and Plan N?', answer: 'Plan G covers Part B coinsurance in full, while Plan N requires small copays for some doctor and ER visits and doesn\'t cover Part B excess charges. Plan N generally carries a lower monthly premium in exchange for those small out-of-pocket costs.' },
  { question: 'What is the Medigap 6-month enrollment window?', answer: 'Your Medigap Open Enrollment Period is the 6 months starting the month you\'re both 65 or older and enrolled in Part B. During this window, insurers must sell you any Medigap policy they offer regardless of health history, without medical underwriting.' },
  { question: 'Does Medigap include prescription drug coverage?', answer: 'No. Medigap plans don\'t include drug coverage, so most people pair a Medigap policy with a separate Part D plan.' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Medicare Supplement (Medigap) Plans Explained',
    description: 'Original Medicare covers about 80% of costs. Learn how Medigap fills the gap, how Plan G compares to Plan N, and the 6-month window you can\'t afford to miss.',
    speakable: {
      '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification',
      cssSelector: ['.quick-answer'],
    },
    ...authorship(DATE_PUBLISHED, DATE_MODIFIED),
  },
  dataset({
    name: 'Medicare Supplement (Medigap) Plan Structure',
    description: 'Official CMS guidance on standardized Medigap plan letters, benefits, and the Medigap Open Enrollment Period.',
    creatorUrl: 'https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap',
  }),
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Medicare Supplement (Medigap) Plans Explained | Learn Medicare"
  description="Original Medicare covers about 80% of costs. Learn how Medigap fills the gap, compare Plan G vs Plan N, and understand your 6-month enrollment window."
  path="/medigap-guide/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Medigap Guide', url: '/medigap-guide/' }]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <BreadcrumbNav crumbs={[{ name: 'Medigap Guide', url: '/medigap-guide/' }]} />
  <PageHero
    label="Medigap"
    heading="Original Medicare Covers 80%. What About the Other 20%?"
    subtext="Medicare Supplement insurance, better known as Medigap, is built to pay the deductibles, coinsurance, and copayments Original Medicare leaves behind."
  >
    <AuthorByline date={DATE_MODIFIED} datePublished={DATE_PUBLISHED} />
  </PageHero>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="quick-answer">
        <p class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</p>
        <p>
          Medigap is standardized supplemental insurance that pays some or all of the out-of-pocket
          costs Original Medicare doesn't — like deductibles, coinsurance, and copayments. Plans
          are labeled by letter (like Plan G or Plan N), and your best window to buy one without
          medical underwriting is a 6-month period around your 65th birthday.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>How Medigap Fills the Gaps</h2>
      <p>
        Original Medicare (Parts A and B) generally covers about 80% of approved costs for the
        services it covers, leaving you responsible for deductibles, coinsurance, and copayments —
        with no annual out-of-pocket maximum. A Medigap policy is designed specifically to pick up
        some or all of that remaining 20%, depending on the plan letter you choose. Because Medigap
        plans are standardized by the federal government, the core benefits of a given plan letter,
        like Plan G, are identical no matter which insurance company sells it — only the premium and
        service tend to differ between carriers.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem;">Plan G vs Plan N — Which Is Right for You?</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Feature</th>
              <th>Plan G</th>
              <th>Plan N</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Part B coinsurance</td>
              <td>Covered in full</td>
              <td>Covered, except small copays for some office/ER visits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Part B excess charges</td>
              <td>Covered in full</td>
              <td>Not covered</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Monthly premium</td>
              <td>Generally higher</td>
              <td>Generally lower</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Predictability</td>
              <td>Very few surprise costs</td>
              <td>Small, predictable copays</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
        Neither plan is universally "better" — Plan G trades a higher premium for fewer surprises,
        while Plan N trades a lower premium for a few small, predictable copays. The right fit
        depends on how you weigh monthly cost against year-round predictability. For a broader look
        at Medigap next to Medicare Advantage, see
        <a href="/coverage">Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem;">The 6-Month Window You Can't Afford to Miss</h2>
      <p>
        Your <strong>Medigap Open Enrollment Period</strong> is a 6-month window that starts the
        month you're both 65 or older and enrolled in Part B. During this window, insurers must
        sell you any Medigap policy they offer, at the best available rate, regardless of your
        health history — no medical underwriting. Miss this window, and an insurer can generally
        use medical underwriting to charge you more, add exclusions, or deny coverage outright
        based on your health. See <a href="/turning-65">what to know about turning 65</a> for how
        this window lines up with your broader Medicare timeline.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem;">Carriers We Work With</h2>
      <p>
        We work with a range of competitive carriers so you can compare rates for the same
        standardized plan letter, including Physicians Mutual, Wellabe, Humana, and other
        Medigap carriers available in your area.
      </p>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <p style="margin:0;">
          This page is educational only and not a recommendation of any specific plan or carrier.
          Rates and carrier availability vary by state and change over time. You can review
          official Medigap details anytime at
          <a href="https://www.medicare.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Medicare.gov</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqs} />

  <section class="section-alt cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner">
      <h2>Get a Free Medigap Rate Comparison</h2>
      <p>Compare Medigap rates across carriers with Kayla Price, a licensed insurance agent (NPN 18530055) — free, with no obligation.</p>
      <a href={BOOKING_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-primary">Schedule Your Free Rate Comparison</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .cta-band { text-align: center; }
  .cta-inner p { color: var(--color-text-muted); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; max-width: 560px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; }
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }

  .quick-answer {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-purple);
    background: var(--color-lavender);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .quick-answer { background: rgba(144, 89, 181, 0.1); }
  .quick-answer-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--color-purple);
  }
  .quick-answer p:last-child { margin: 0; }
</style>
